        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.106,
            0.724,
            0.155
          ],
          "content": "(v) $( 9 7 ) ^ { 2 }$ (vi) $( 1 8 \\times 2 9 )$ (vii) $( 3 4 \\times 4 3 )$ (viii) $( 2 0 5 ) ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.165,
            0.808,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": "3. Factor the following: (i) $9a^{2}+b^{2}+4c^{2}-6ab+12ac-4bc$ (ii) $1 6 s ^ { 2 } + 2 5 t ^ { 2 } - 4 0 s t$ (iii) $r ^ { 2 }   -   r   -   4 2$ (iv) $4 9 g ^ { 2 } + 1 4 g h + h ^ { 2 }$ (v) $6 4 u ^ { 2 } + 1 2 1 v ^ { 2 } + 4 w ^ { 2 } - 1 7 6 u v - 3 2 u w + 4 4 v w$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.303,
            0.398,
            0.327
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.34,
            0.854,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": "James and Reshma were talking about algebraic identities they learnt in school."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.383,
            0.586,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": "James: $(a - b)^{2} \\left( a + b \\right) = (a^{2} - 2ab + b^{2})(a + b)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.405,
            0.844,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": "Reshma: I have a different idea. $(a - b)^{2} (a + b) = (a - b) \\left[ (a - b) (a + b) \\right]$ (a – b)(a<sup>2</sup> – b<sup>2</sup>)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.454,
            0.57,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": "I will find this product to get the answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.476,
            0.554,
            0.496
          ],
          "content": "According to you, who is correct and why?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.5,
            0.702,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "Try to combine more such identities and find new results."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.561,
            0.5,
            0.584
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.7 Finding New Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.595,
            0.886,
            0.634
          ],
          "content": "In this section we will play around with algebraic expressions to see if we can arrive at new identities."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.638,
            0.593,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "What do you think $(a + b)^{3}$ will look like?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.664,
            0.783,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "Let us find out the answer using the distributive property:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.689,
            0.79,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "(a+b)^{3}=(a+b)(a^{2}+2ab+b^{2})=a^{3}+3a^{2}b+3ab^{2}+b^{3}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.714,
            0.63,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "Here we have found a new identity, namely,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.237,
            0.739,
            0.554,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "(a + b)^{3} = a^{3} + 3a^{2}b + 3ab^{2} + b^{3}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.765,
            0.667,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": "Let us try to visualise this identity. We saw that a square of side $a \\; + \\; b$ can be divided into squares and rectangles. This led us to the identity, $(a^{2}+b)^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}$ . What if we have a cube of edge $a + b ?$ Can we divide a cube of edge $( a + b )$ into smaller cubes and cuboids and represent this new identity?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.717,
            0.769,
            0.881,
            0.885
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.668,
                0.892,
                0.92,
                0.91
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.9: A cube of edge a + b"
            }
          ],
